            <div xml:id="d121">

                <pb facs="0211" n="113" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg113"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002 Manchuria/107: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-06-27T14:00:00+08:00">June 27, 1932—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 27—1:24 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>720. Your 173, June 24, 2 p.m. My British colleague has received instructions
                    similar to yours in paragraph 1. However, he is authorized privately and
                    informally to continue in his conversations with <pb facs="0212" n="114"
                        xml:id="pg114"/> the Chinese to emphasize the importance of making every
                    effort to prevent the disruption of the Maritime Customs Service.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
